Meier","name":"Meier, Florian","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34362889"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1038/s41467-021-24985-x","issn":["20411723"],"journal":"Nature Communications","paperid":"4772","status":"p","title":"Nature Communications","volume":"12"},"review":"No Review","scoID":["2-s2.0-85112632233"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8ca69492-4ece-4bbb-9729-435ecab27df6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Reply to \"Quality control requirements for the correct annotation of lipidomics data\"","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-477548","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Today’s smartphone users often use several mobile messaging services alongside each other, even though they typically offer the same features and functionality. Where previous studies have focused on how and why users permanently abandon mobile messaging services and switch to new ones, this study examines the degree to which smartphone users keep switching back and forth between multiple services, and the factors that influence this temporary switching behavior. We used an exploratory research approach in a longitudinal diary study combined with semi-structured interviews. We found that temporary switching behavior is influenced by technological affordances, contextual factors, individual preferences, and the type of conversation. Both positive and negative impacts were identified within these aspects, with some having an indirect influence, revealing the complexity of temporary switching behavior.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Meier, Florian Maximilian","Langberg Schmidt, Amalie","Bogers, Toine"],"cuid":["S02-082.14-715612"],"event":{"acronym":"iConference","end":"2021-03-31","place":"Beijing, cn","start":"2021-03-28","title":"iConference 2021","year":"2021"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Contextual factors","Longitudinal diary study","Mobile messaging services","Technological affordances","Temporary switching behavior"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"they each have their forte an exploratory diary study of temporary switching behavior between mobile messenger services","src":["aau","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/398098837/iConf2021_MMSwitching.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S02-082.14-715612","identifier":[{"id":"85104851190","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-279683fb-6ce2-4723-9cdb-8597ff48a855","itu-6a9af10e-057a-4909-aa7b-94efff001634"],"keyword":["Contextual factors","Longitudinal diary study","Mobile messaging services","Technological affordances","Temporary switching behavior"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C0F367C048D6D6570D471BDB8DBAA0E5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/398098837/iConf2021_MMSwitching.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Humanities (HUM)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Science, Policy and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NEtwoRks, Data, and Society"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Florian Maximilian","key":"meierpau","last":"Meier","locid":"30784c17-7dc1-48de-9a36-f6e17f329de0","name":"Meier, Florian Maximilian","orcid":"0000-0001-9408-0686","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Amalie","key":"langbergschmidtpau","last":"Langberg Schmidt","locid":"f5660082-9c82-48fe-9ba8-3a794401b097","name":"Langberg Schmidt, Amalie","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Toine","key":"bogerspau","last":"Bogers","locid":"c39fa087-a00a-425e-9e0f-7fa301dbe554","name":"Bogers, Toine","orcid":"0000-0003-0716-676X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Katharina","key":"toeppepedh","last":"Toeppe","name":"Toeppe, Katharina","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Hui","key":"yanpedh","last":"Yan","name":"Yan, Hui","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Samuel","key":"kaiwahchupedh","last":"Kai Wah Chu","name":"Kai Wah Chu, Samuel","rank":6,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-71292-1_22","eisbn":["9783030712921"],"isbn":["9783030712914"],"issn":["03029743"],"pages":"268-286","publisher":"Springer","series":"Lecture Notes in Computer Science","status":"p","subtitle":"16th International Conference, iConference 2021, Beijing, China, March 17–31, 2021, Proceedings, Part I","title":"Diversity, Divergence, Dialogue"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85104851190"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-279683fb-6ce2-4723-9cdb-8597ff48a855","itu-6a9af10e-057a-4909-aa7b-94efff001634"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"“They each have their forte”: An Exploratory Diary Study of Temporary Switching Behavior between Mobile Messenger Services","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-082.14-715612","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Socially responsible behavior is crucial for slowing the spread of infectious diseases. However, economic and epidemiological models of disease transmission abstract from prosocial motivations as a driver of behaviors that impact the health of others. In an incentivized study, we show that a large majority of people are very reluctant to put others at risk for their personal benefit. Moreover, this experimental measure of prosociality predicts health behaviors during the COVID-19 pandemic, measured in a separate and ostensibly unrelated study with the same people. Prosocial individuals are more likely to follow physical distancing guidelines, stay home when sick, and buy face masks. We also find that prosociality measured two years before the pandemic predicts health behaviors during the pandemic. Our findings indicate that prosociality is a stable, long-term predictor of policy-relevant behaviors, suggesting that the impact of policies on a population may depend on the degree of prosociality.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Campos-Mercade, Pol","Meier, Armando","Schneider, Florian","Wengström, Erik"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-716387"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Basel","University of Lausanne"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Externalities","Faculty of Social Sciences","Health Behavior","Prosociality","Social preferences"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prosociality predicts health behaviors during the covid 000000000019 pandemic","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpubeco.2021.104367","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-716387","identifier":[{"id":"PMC7842154","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85100471711","type":"scopus"},{"id":"33531719","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-46eae271-6c6c-4c98-8513-ce6ab7446d1f","ku-67dc4680-66da-4ed0-90be-9a9ab0d89cf9"],"keyword":["CoVID-19","Externalities","Faculty of Social Sciences","Social preferences","health behavior","prosociality"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-3AA5E143EC5142800DADDE908CB41C99"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpubeco.2021.104367","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/322270829/Prosociality_predicts_health.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Economics, Department of Economics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of lausanne","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lausanne","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ch","key":"university of basel","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Basel","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Pol","key":"camposmercadepau","last":"Campos-Mercade","locid":"404b6d52-5463-46b4-88d4-064b3a1167b0","name":"Campos-Mercade, Pol","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Armando N.","key":"meierpau","last":"Meier","name":"Meier, Armando N.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Florian H.","key":"schneiderpau","last":"Schneider","locid":"214a624d-2f75-4637-8672-48e63f5bb2f5","name":"Schneider, Florian H.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik","key":"wengströmpau","last":"Wengström","locid":"338b0198-6eee-45a9-bc74-fbfc11593edf","name":"Wengström, Erik Roland","name_org":"Wengström, Erik","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["33531719"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.jpubeco.2021.104367","issn":["00472727"],"journal":"Journal of Public Economics","paperid":"104367","status":"p","title":"Journal of Public Economics","volume":"195"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85100471711"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-46eae271-6c6c-4c98-8513-ce6ab7446d1f","ku-67dc4680-66da-4ed0-90be-9a9ab0d89cf9"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Prosociality predicts health behaviors during the COVID-19 pandemic","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-716387","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The stalling of COVID-19 vaccination rates threatens public health. To increase vaccination rates, governments across the world are considering the use of monetary incentives. Here we present evidence about the effect of guaranteed payments on COVID-19 vaccination uptake. We ran a large preregistered randomized controlled trial (with 8286 participants) in Sweden and linked the data to population-wide administrative vaccination records. We found that modest monetary payments of 24 US dollars (200 Swedish kronor) increased vaccination rates by 4.2 percentage points (P = 0.005), from a baseline rate of 71.6%. By contrast, behavioral nudges increased stated intentions to become vaccinated but had only small and not statistically significant impacts on vaccination rates. The results highlight the potential of modest monetary incentives to raise vaccination rates.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Campos-Mercade, Pol","Schneider, Florian","Meier, Armando","Meier, Stephan","Pope, Devin","Wengström, Erik"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-716394"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["Switzerland","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Columbia University","National Bureau of Economic Research","The University of Chicago","University of Basel","University of Lausanne"],"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"monetary incentives increase covid 000000000019 vaccinations","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abm0475","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-716394","identifier":[{"id":"85117991238","type":"scopus"},{"id":"34618594","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-4ca20f57-937f-46a1-bea6-9725dc1c346b","ku-4e7d7bbd-d685-42cb-b472-264cc6560ed7"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-C76E3D46FA1859FFDCC678948184A9F2"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/313701370/science.abm0475.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1126/science.abm0475","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Social Science (SOC)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Social Sciences, Department of Economics, Department of Economics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"ch","key":"university of lausanne","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Lausanne","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"ch","key":"university of basel","loc":["Switzerland","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Basel","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"columbia university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Columbia University","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"the university of chicago","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"The University of Chicago","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"us","key":"national bureau of economic research","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"National Bureau of Economic Research","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Pol","key":"camposmercadepolpau","last":"Campos-Mercade","locid":"404b6d52-5463-46b4-88d4-064b3a1167b0","name":"Campos-Mercade, Pol","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Florian","key":"schneiderflorianpau","last":"Schneider","name":"Schneider, Florian","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Armando N.","key":"meierarmandonpau","last":"Meier","name":"Meier, Armando N.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Florian H.","key":"schneiderflorianhpau","last":"Schneider","locid":"214a624d-2f75-4637-8672-48e63f5bb2f5","name":"Schneider, Florian H.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Armando","key":"meierarmandopau","last":"Meier","name":"Meier, Armando","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Stephan","key":"meierstephanpau","last":"Meier","name":"Meier, Stephan","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5 6","first":"Devin","key":"popedevinpau","last":"Pope","name":"Pope, Devin","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Erik","key":"wengströmerikrolandpau","last":"Wengström","locid":"338b0198-6eee-45a9-bc74-fbfc11593edf","name":"Wengström, Erik Roland","name_org":"Wengström, Erik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Erik","key":"wengströmerikpau","last":"Wengström","name":"Wengström, Erik","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["34618594"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1126/science.abm0475","issn":["00368075","10959203"],"issn_org":["00368075"],"issue":"6569","journal":"Science","pages":"879-882","status":"p","title":"Science","volume":"374"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85117991238"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-4ca20f57-937f-46a1-bea6-9725dc1c346b","ku-4e7d7bbd-d685-42cb-b472-264cc6560ed7"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Monetary incentives increase COVID-19 vaccinations","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-716394","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"The Scandinavian countries are often seen as a unity. However, during the COVID-19 pandemic striking differences on how the countries approached the crisis became evident. This quantitative-ethnographic (QE) study aimed to understand political and cultural similarities and differences between the three Scandinavian countries -Denmark, Norway and Sweden - through their crisis communications during the COVID-19 pandemic. Specifically, we focused on how the health authorities of the three countries, in their press releases, treated information about COVID-19 and acted in four fields: reorganization of population behavior, containment of viral transmission, preparation of health systems, and management of socioeconomic impacts. As a methodology, the QE tools nCoder and ENA were applied, respectively: to code the press releases and to correlate the treatment of information with the four fields of action.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Schnaider, Karoline","Schiavetto, Stefano","Meier, Florian Maximilian","Wasson, Barbara","Allsopp, Benjamin Brink","Spikol, Daniel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-078633"],"event":{"end":"2020-10-27","start":"2020-10-24","title":"2nd International Conference on Quantitative Ethnography","year":"2020"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, South","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Brazil","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Malmö University","Umeå University","Universidade Estadual de Campinas","University of Bergen"],"keyword":["Covid 19","Crisis communication","Pandemic","Quantitative ethnography"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"governmental response to the covid 000000000019 pandemic a quantitative ethnographic comparison of public health authorities communication in denmark norway and sweden","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-078633","identifier":[{"id":"85089519982","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-62849783-10ce-4588-8308-1a641757ada6"],"keyword":["COVID-19","Crisis communication","Pandemic","Quantitative ethnography"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D963F9CBF6BB37EBE626C2FBA1787D31"],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Humanities (HUM)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"umeå university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Umeå University","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"br","key":"universidade estadual de campinas","loc":["Brazil","America, South"],"name":"Universidade Estadual de Campinas","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Humanities","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Social Sciences and Humanities, Department of Communication and Psychology, Science, Policy and Information Studies"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"no","key":"university of bergen","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Bergen","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"se","key":"malmö university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Malmö University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Karoline","key":"schnaiderpau","last":"Schnaider","name":"Schnaider, Karoline","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Stefano","key":"schiavettopau","last":"Schiavetto","name":"Schiavetto, Stefano","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Florian Maximilian","key":"meierpau","last":"Meier","locid":"30784c17-7dc1-48de-9a36-f6e17f329de0","name":"Meier, Florian Maximilian","orcid":"0000-0001-9408-0686","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Barbara","key":"wassonpau","last":"Wasson","name":"Wasson, Barbara","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Benjamin","key":"brinkallsopppau","last":"Brink Allsopp","locid":"d3d63ab7-23f1-4b1e-bc2f-f34ec09c5a9f","name":"Allsopp, Benjamin Brink","name_org":"Brink Allsopp, Benjamin","orcid":"0000-0002-6992-9171","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"5","corresponding":null,"first":"Daniel","key":"spikolpau","last":"Spikol","name":"Spikol, Daniel","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-67788-6_28","eisbn":["9783030677886"],"isbn":["9783030677879"],"issn":["18650929"],"pages":"406-421","publisher":"Springer","series":"Communications in Computer and Information Science","status":"p","subtitle":"Second International Conference, ICQE 2020, Malibu, CA, USA, February 1-3, 2021, Proceedings","title":"Advances in Quantitative Ethnography"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85101372806"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-62849783-10ce-4588-8308-1a641757ada6"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Governmental Response to the COVID-19 Pandemic : A Quantitative Ethnographic Comparison of Public Health Authorities' Communication in Denmark, Norway, and Sweden","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-078633","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Aims: Understanding fine-grain diversity patterns across large spatial extents is fundamental for macroecological research and biodiversity conservation. Using the GrassPlot database, we provide benchmarks of fine-grain richness values of Palaearctic open habitats for vascular plants, bryophytes, lichens and complete vegetation (i.e., the sum of the former three groups). Location: Palaearctic biogeographic realm. Methods: We used 126,524 plots of eight standard grain sizes from the GrassPlot database: 0.0001, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 1, 10, 100 and 1,000 m2 and calculated the mean richness and standard deviations, as well as maximum, minimum, median, and first and third quartiles for each combination of grain size, taxonomic group, biome, region, vegetation type and phytosociological class. Results: Patterns of plant diversity in vegetation types and biomes differ across grain sizes and taxonomic groups. Overall, secondary (mostly semi-natural) grasslands and natural grasslands are the richest vegetation type. The open-access file ”GrassPlot Diversity Benchmarks” and the web tool “GrassPlot Diversity Explorer” are now available online (https://edgg.org/databases/GrasslandDiversityExplorer) and provide more insights into species richness patterns in the Palaearctic open habitats. Conclusions: The GrassPlot Diversity Benchmarks provide high-quality data on species richness in open habitat types across the Palaearctic. These benchmark data can be used in vegetation ecology, macroecology, biodiversity conservation and data quality checking. While the amount of data in the underlying GrassPlot database and their spatial coverage are smaller than in other extensive vegetation-plot databases, species recordings in GrassPlot are on average more complete, making it a valuable complementary data source in macroecology.","author_count":223,"contributors":["Biurrun, Idoia","Pielech, Remigiusz","Dembicz, Iwona","Gillet, François","Kozub, Łukasz","Marcenò, Corrado","Reitalu